  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Direct absolute oral bioavailability measurements for polar nucleoside analogs in L1/L2 are at or above the 20% threshold: zalcitabine 80% in fasted HIV patients (C15) and 30% in fasted mice (C39); ddA/ddI 38% in fasting patients (C12) and ddI 30–54% (C52); OGT719 about 25% in patients (C10). The query is a carbocyclic guanine nucleoside lacking the acid-labile N-glycosidic bond of ddA/ddI, so these values support F ≥ 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C15","C39","C12","C52","C10"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records for entecavir, a carbocyclic guanine nucleoside, show rapid oral absorption (Tmax 0.63–1.0 h; C61, C84), and oral famciclovir produces measurable penciclovir exposure (C82, C72). No L3 record affirmatively indicates F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C61","C84","C82","C72"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 solubility/dissolution evidence for related nucleosides is favorable: ribavirin high solubility (C110, C118), ganciclovir 2.6 mg/mL (C133), aciclovir fast dissolution (C143), and AZT >90% GI absorption (C121). Low passive permeability records are not affirmative low-F evidence because didanosine PAMPA 0.11×10^-6 cm/s (C126) coexists with measured F 30–54% (C52).",
      "card_ids": ["C110","C118","C133","C143","C121","C126","C52"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall evidence shows that nucleoside transporters mediate intestinal uptake of related nucleosides: cladribine via ENT/CNT (C183, C192, C206), gemcitabine via nucleoside transporters (C181), ribavirin via CNT3 (C190), and trifluridine via CNT1 (C184). Lamivudine is not a BCRP substrate and shows no basal ABC-transporter efflux (C191, C165). Efflux observed for abacavir, cladribine, and ddI coexists with F ≥ 20% in those or closely related contexts (C15, C39, C12, C52).",
      "card_ids": ["C183","C192","C206","C181","C190","C184","C191","C165","C195","C160","C204","C175","C161","C182","C176","C15","C39","C12","C52"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic-stability records for related nucleosides are low-to-moderate and not threshold-negative: AZT human liver microsome CLint values are about 0.66–3.79 µL/min/mg (C219, C243, C229, C231, C256, C247, C234, C223) with a predicted hepatic clearance of 1.7 mL/min/kg (C210, C248); lamivudine rat hepatic clearance is 15.7 mL/min/kg (C246); theophylline hepatic clearance is low (C237, C245). High aldehyde-oxidase clearance values for 6-deoxypenciclovir (C224, C218, C251, C252) reflect prodrug activation of a different scaffold and are not applicable to the guanine carbocyclic query.",
      "card_ids": ["C219","C243","C229","C231","C256","C247","C234","C223","C210","C248","C246","C237","C245","C224","C218","C251","C252"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": [
        "C219","C243","C229","C231","C256","C247","C234","C223",
        "C210","C248","C246","C237","C245"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct measured absolute oral bioavailability for the exact query molecule.",
    "No human pharmacokinetic data for the cyclobutyl bis(hydroxymethyl)guanine scaffold.",
    "No direct hepatic metabolism or clearance data for the query; L6 records are for other nucleoside analogs and prodrugs.",
    "No direct intestinal metabolism or deamination data for the query.",
    "Formulation and dose are not specified beyond the fasted state.",
    "The magnitude of nucleoside-transporter affinity or efflux for the query is not directly measured."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic-stability evidence is mechanistically supportive rather than threshold-negative: related nucleoside analogs show low-to-moderate intrinsic/hepatic clearance, and the high aldehyde-oxidase clearance records for 6-deoxypenciclovir reflect prodrug activation of a different scaffold. Combined with direct F ≥ 20% for polar nucleosides, favorable solubility/dissolution, transporter-mediated uptake, and oral exposure records, the prediction remains high with moderate confidence."
}

